## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where ALL content (236+ consumers via useContent) is sourced and provided to the React tree. It currently only passes through static defaultContent from getStaticProps with no live preview integration — no Contentstack SDK live_preview config, no URL query param handling (live_preview hash, content_type_uid), and no client-side listener for preview edits. Every component that reads content gets it from this context, so this is the computation/source site, not a display site.
  - **Minimal fix:** In ContentstackProvider, integrate Contentstack Live Preview: (1) read live_preview query params from the URL (live_preview, content_type_uid, live_preview_hash) using Next.js router, (2) when those params are present, use the Contentstack SDK's live preview query mode (pass live_preview config to the Stack initialization or to individual queries) to fetch draft/preview content instead of published, (3) add a client-side event listener (Contentstack SDK's onLivePreviewUpdate or equivalent) that merges incoming preview edits into the context's content object in real-time. The minimal change is to replace the static `content: defaultContent` with a computed value that merges defaultContent with any active live preview data, and to add a useEffect that subscribes to live preview events and updates state accordingly. The Contentstack SDK's live preview utilities from @metrolinx/cx-shared or contentstack-sdk should be reused if available — check the cx-shared package for a livePreview helper or Stack factory that already includes live_preview config.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When an editor changes a text field in the CMS preview panel, the corresponding text on the rendered page updates to show the new value without the tester manually refreshing the browser
- When an editor changes card content (title, description, or link) in the CMS preview panel, the rendered card on the page displays the updated content without a manual page reload
- When an editor replaces an image in the CMS preview panel, the new image appears on the rendered page in place of the old one without a manual page reload
- When an editor modifies table content in the CMS preview panel, the rendered table on the page displays the updated cell values without a manual page reload
- A visitor opening the site as a normal (non-preview) page sees the published content rendered correctly, with no preview toolbars, no error messages, and no unexpected content changes
- When a page is opened via a preview link from the CMS, the initial render displays the editor's latest draft content rather than stale or previously published content
- When an editor removes a content field in the CMS, the rendered page displays a sensible fallback or empty state in place of that content, with no blank screen, broken layout, or visible errors

